# Touch bring-up for HUAWEI Honor 9A (MOA-LX9N) TWRP (source-built omni tree).
# Touch IC is an Ilitek ILI9881H TDDI (project Y163791300) behind Huawei's THP
# "hostprocessing": the kernel driver only feeds frames to userspace, and
# aptouch_daemon + libthp + libafehal + libtsa do the touch decoding.
# Lives in /system/etc/init so AOSP init always parses it, regardless of
# ro.hardware. The ramdisk-patching path uses the same content, see
# tools/release_overlay/aptouch_daemon.rc.
#
# LK merges the stock recovery_vendor ramdisk into ours (lib/ramdisk_merge), so
# /vendor already holds aptouch_daemon, libthp, libtsa_v1 and libafehalY16379*.
#
# The two things that actually make touch work here (verified on-device):
#   - hwservicemanager.ready: libthp does a HIDL getService for the sensor
#     service and libhidlbase polls that property forever ("Waited for
#     hwservicemanager.ready for a second, waiting another..."), so the daemon
#     hangs in startListening and never opens /dev/thp (thp_status stuck at
#     0x1). TWRP has no hwservicemanager; setting the property lets the lookup
#     fail harmlessly and the daemon proceeds (thp_status 0xc1). The daemon
#     runs in "upgrade mode" here, where it disables binder/vndbinder anyway.
#   - LD_LIBRARY_PATH: init exports 64-bit /sbin globally in the ported
#     ramdisk, which makes 32-bit vendor binaries fail to link. Setting it per
#     service keeps aptouch_daemon on the 32-bit libs.
# The recovery kernel boots with androidboot.selinux=enforcing, hence seclabel.

on early-init
    setprop hwservicemanager.ready true

on init
    symlink /vendor /system/vendor

on boot
    setprop thp.service_enable 1
    setprop odm.thp.service_enable 1
    start aptouch

service aptouch /vendor/bin/aptouch_daemon
    user root
    group root
    setenv LD_LIBRARY_PATH /system/lib:/vendor/lib
    seclabel u:r:recovery:s0

on property:thp.service_enable=0
    stop aptouch

on property:odm.thp.service_enable=0
    stop aptouch
